    {
      "id": "OSrxEo_8uCAwSDvqZvyyp",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 858.53125,
      "y": 1245.95703125,
      "width": 915.159423828125,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2p",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 28072430,
      "version": 614,
      "versionNonce": 407806356,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785905824330,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n-- breaking crawler service into stage:\n1. URL fetcher: fetches the HTML of the web page from the external server. \nIf there is a failure, we can retry the fetch without losing progress on the rest of the data.\nStore HTML in blobs for further processing\n---------------------\n2. text & URL extraction: extract metadata ==> DynamoDB for storing metadata\n-- parsing Queue identify by URL_id as starter. (SQS)\n-- To implement exponential backoff, we use the ChangeMessageVisibility API \nto dynamically adjust the timeout based on the ApproximateReceiveCount attribute, \nwhich tracks how many times a message has been received. \nFor example, after each failed fetch, we calculate the next visibility timeout as \nand call ChangeMessageVisibility to set it. \nThis keeps the message hidden until we're ready to retry.\n-- when crawler goes down;\n--- SQS in frontier (or Kafka) for retry purpose\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n-- breaking crawler service into stage:\n1. URL fetcher: fetches the HTML of the web page from the external server. \nIf there is a failure, we can retry the fetch without losing progress on the rest of the data.\nStore HTML in blobs for further processing\n---------------------\n2. text & URL extraction: extract metadata ==> DynamoDB for storing metadata\n-- parsing Queue identify by URL_id as starter. (SQS)\n-- To implement exponential backoff, we use the ChangeMessageVisibility API \nto dynamically adjust the timeout based on the ApproximateReceiveCount attribute, \nwhich tracks how many times a message has been received. \nFor example, after each failed fetch, we calculate the next visibility timeout as \nand call ChangeMessageVisibility to set it. \nThis keeps the message hidden until we're ready to retry.\n-- when crawler goes down;\n--- SQS in frontier (or Kafka) for retry purpose\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "-rBgejTX_ojBwfAC1Xf1a",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 845.51171875,
      "y": 1715.578125,
      "width": 1104.19921875,
      "height": 200,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2q",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1850575854,
      "version": 168,
      "versionNonce": 2038665772,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785905830226,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n-- robots.txt is a file that websites use to communicate with web crawlers. \nIt tells crawlers which pages they are allowed to crawl and which pages they are not. \nIt also tells crawlers how frequently they can crawl the site. An example of a robots.txt file might look like this:\n\nUser-agent: * \nDisallow: /private/\nCrawl-delay: 10",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n-- robots.txt is a file that websites use to communicate with web crawlers. \nIt tells crawlers which pages they are allowed to crawl and which pages they are not. \nIt also tells crawlers how frequently they can crawl the site. An example of a robots.txt file might look like this:\n\nUser-agent: * \nDisallow: /private/\nCrawl-delay: 10",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "yoyyU6tHWKn8F77lBNT5z",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1259,
      "y": 1745.5,
      "width": 8,
      "height": 50,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2r",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1152335026,
      "version": 8,
      "versionNonce": 1461053230,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782244187442,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
